      "state": {
        "stepPhase": "show t+s",
        "swapOrder": "s then t",
        "showMatrices": true
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Chaining translations is nothing more than adding their vectors — two displacements always collapse into one.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two equal-width panels filling the full canvas, divided by a thin light vertical rule; the drawing (axes, squares, arrows) occupies the entire width — no inline matrix column. Each panel has its own origin toward the lower-left with a thin black x-axis arrow right and y-axis arrow up, italic serif 'x' and 'y' at the arrowheads. Squares are solid orange (~#F5901E) with thick black outlines, all identical, roughly one-sixth of a panel's width; the origin square is centered on the origin so both axes cross it. Vectors are thick bright red arrows with solid triangular heads; labels 't', 's', 't+s' in large bold serif with a white halo, sized to stay legible when the figure is rendered at about half scale. Left panel: origin square, arrow t up-right at ~40 degrees, translated square. Right panel: origin square, arrow t to the intermediate square, shorter arrow s pointing down-right to the final square, and the long shallow arrow t+s from origin to final square forming a clear triangle. Small red circular handles at the tips of t and s. No dashed component guides, no grey caption, no extra annotation text in the drawing area. Stage stepping only dims/hides elements in the RIGHT panel; the left panel always stays fully opaque. Numeric readouts live in the surrounding UI panel, and the 3x3 homogeneous matrices appear only in an overlay popup toggled from the UI, rendered at comfortably readable size with the translation column tinted red."
}

